    $astro3_master$> We have read the *daily* sky (the shadow of Earth's spin) and the *yearly* sky (the shadow of Earth's tilt). Now the sky's third great clock — the *monthly* one — and the object that keeps it: the Moon. The Moon is the most dramatic and changeable thing overhead, cycling through its **phases** — new, crescent, quarter, gibbous, full, and back — over about a month, a rhythm so reliable it gave us the very word "month" (from "moon") and structured human calendars for millennia. And once again the appearance hides a simple geometry, and once again there is a tempting wrong explanation to clear away. The phases are *not*, as many people believe, the Earth's shadow falling on the Moon — that would be a lunar eclipse, a rare event, not the monthly cycle. They have nothing to do with any shadow. They are the shadow, in this course's deeper sense, of one beautifully simple fact: **the Moon is a sphere, the Sun lights exactly half of it at all times — one hemisphere always in daylight, one always in night — and as the Moon orbits the Earth we view that permanently half-lit ball from continuously changing angles, so we see a changing *fraction* of the lit half.** A crescent is the sliver of the lit side we glimpse when the Moon is nearly between us and the Sun; a full Moon is the entire lit face, seen when the Moon is opposite the Sun. What follows builds this geometry completely, dismantles the shadow misconception, uncovers why the phase cycle is longer than the Moon's actual orbit, learns to read the *time of night* from the Moon's phase, and confronts the lovely puzzle of why we *always see the same face* — tidal locking, which turns out to mean not that the Moon doesn't rotate, but that it rotates *exactly once per orbit.* The Moon's ever-changing face is, like everything else here, the shadow of a geometry: a lit sphere, an orbit, and a point of view.

{{image: Lunar phase | The Moon through a lunar cycle — new, crescent, quarter, gibbous, full, and back. The Moon itself is not changing; what changes is how much of its permanently sunlit half turns toward Earth as it orbits. The word "month" descends from "moon," so ancient was this clock.}}

## The same Moon, a different shape

Watch the Moon for a month, and you witness something that no other object in the sky does: it *changes shape.* The stars are fixed points; the Sun is an unvarying disk; the planets are steady dots. But the Moon *transforms*, night by night, through a graceful sequence of shapes. One evening it's a thin crescent hanging low in the west after sunset. A few nights later it's a fatter crescent, then a half-disk (we call it a "quarter," confusingly), then a bulging, lopsided shape (a "gibbous" Moon), until — about two weeks after that first crescent — it's a complete, brilliant, circular *full* Moon, rising in the east as the Sun sets in the west. And then it reverses: the full Moon thins back through gibbous, half, crescent, until it vanishes entirely for a night or two (the "new" Moon), before the cycle begins again. Round and round, about once a month, forever.

What is happening? Why does the Moon, alone among the lights of the sky, cycle through these shapes? For most of human history this was a profound mystery. Even today, ask people what causes the phases of the Moon, and a great many — including, surveys find, a large fraction of well-educated adults — will give an answer that *sounds* reasonable and is completely wrong: *"The phases are the shadow of the Earth falling on the Moon. When the Earth gets between the Sun and Moon, it casts a shadow, and that shadow creeps across the Moon, giving us the phases."*

It's a natural guess. We know the Earth is between the Sun and the Moon sometimes; we know shadows exist; we can see the Moon is partly dark. Surely the dark part is Earth's shadow? **But it is not — and the reasons it cannot be are worth stating right away, because they point us toward the truth.** Here's the first clue that something is wrong with the shadow idea: *you can often see the crescent Moon and the Sun in the sky at the same time.* On many evenings, a thin crescent Moon hangs in the western sky while the Sun is still up or just setting — both visible together. But if the Moon's dark part were Earth's shadow, the Earth would have to be *between* the Sun and the Moon to cast that shadow — and then the Sun and Moon would be on *opposite* sides of us, never visible together. The fact that we routinely see the Sun and a partly-lit Moon in the same sky *proves* the darkness on the Moon is not Earth's shadow.

And here's the second clue: the phases cycle *smoothly and continuously* all the way around, every single month, like clockwork. But the Earth only passes directly between the Sun and Moon *occasionally* — and when it does cast its shadow on the Moon, we get a **lunar eclipse**, a distinct, relatively rare event (a few times a year at most) in which the *full* Moon darkens and often turns coppery red for an hour or two. Lunar eclipses are real, and they *are* the Earth's shadow on the Moon — but they are *not* the phases; they're a separate phenomenon. The monthly phases happen *every* month, relentlessly, whether or not any shadow-casting alignment occurs.

**So the phases are not a shadow at all. They are something more elegant: the changing view of a ball that is always half-lit.** The Moon, like the Earth, is a sphere; the Sun, off to one side, illuminates exactly one hemisphere of it — a permanent "day side" facing the Sun and a permanent "night side" facing away — at all times. What changes is not the lighting of the Moon but *our angle of view* as the Moon orbits the Earth: sometimes we look at the Moon's lit hemisphere nearly head-on (full Moon), sometimes mostly at its dark hemisphere (new Moon), and usually we see some *fraction* of the lit side (crescents and gibbous shapes). The phases are the geometry of a half-lit sphere, seen from a moving vantage point.

## The geometry of phases: a half-lit sphere, seen from a moving point

Once you have the phase geometry, every feature of the monthly Moon follows — its shape, its position relative to the Sun, its rise and set times, everything. The whole thing rests on two facts and one changing quantity.

### The two fixed facts

**Fact one: the Moon is always exactly half-lit.** The Moon is a sphere (a ball of rock), and it shines only by *reflecting sunlight* — it produces no light of its own. The Sun, far off to one side, illuminates whatever hemisphere of the Moon faces it — exactly *half* the Moon's surface, at all times. There is always a lit hemisphere (the half facing the Sun — the Moon's "daytime" side) and a dark hemisphere (the half facing away — its "nighttime" side). This is true *always*, at every moment, regardless of phase — just as the Earth always has a day side and a night side. **The lighting of the Moon never changes: it is eternally half-lit.** What changes is only how much of that lit half we can *see* from Earth.

**Fact two: the Moon orbits the Earth.** The Moon travels around the Earth in an orbit, taking about a month to go around. As it orbits, its *position* relative to the Sun-Earth line continuously changes — sometimes the Moon is roughly between the Earth and the Sun, sometimes off to one side, sometimes on the far side of the Earth from the Sun. This changing position is the key variable.

### The changing quantity: our viewing angle

Now put the two facts together. The Moon is always half-lit, and it orbits the Earth. As it orbits, *we on Earth view the Moon's lit hemisphere from continuously changing angles.* And how much of the lit half we can see depends entirely on the *angle* between the Sun, the Moon, and us — on where the Moon is in its orbit. Walk through the orbit:

- When the Moon is **between the Earth and the Sun**: the Moon's lit hemisphere faces *away* from us, toward the Sun, and its dark hemisphere faces us. We see (almost) none of the lit side — the Moon is dark, essentially invisible. This is the **new Moon.**

- When the Moon is **off to one side** (so the Sun-Moon-Earth angle is 90°): we see the lit hemisphere *edge-on* — half of the lit side and half of the dark side face us, so the Moon appears as a *half-disk* (one half bright, one half dark). This is a **quarter Moon** (first or last/third quarter). It's called "quarter" not because we see a quarter of the disk — we see half the disk — but because the Moon is a *quarter* of the way through its cycle.

- When the Moon is **on the far side of the Earth from the Sun**: the Moon's lit hemisphere faces *directly toward us*, fully. We see the entire lit half — a complete bright disk. This is the **full Moon.**

- At the **in-between angles**, we see in-between fractions: a *crescent* (less than half the disk lit, when the Moon is between new and quarter — we're seeing a sliver of the lit side), or a *gibbous* Moon (more than half lit, between quarter and full — we're seeing most of the lit side).

```moonphase
```

**That is the entire phase cycle: as the Moon orbits, the angle between Sun, Moon, and Earth sweeps from 0° (new) through 90° (quarter) to 180° (full) and back, and we see a correspondingly changing fraction of the always-lit half — from none (new) to all (full) and back.** The phases are not the Moon changing; they are *our view* of the unchanging half-lit Moon changing, as we watch its lit hemisphere from a revolving vantage point.

### Waxing and waning; which side is lit

Two refinements complete the picture. As the Moon goes from new to full (its lit fraction *growing*), we say it is **waxing**; from full back to new (lit fraction *shrinking*), it is **waning.** So the full cycle runs: new → waxing crescent → first quarter → waxing gibbous → full → waning gibbous → last (third) quarter → waning crescent → new.

And notice *which side* of the Moon is lit: the lit portion always bulges toward the Sun, because that's the direction the light comes from. A waxing crescent in the evening sky has its lit edge on the side toward the (just-set) Sun, in the west. This is why the crescent Moon's "horns" point *away* from the Sun, and why you can tell, from a crescent's orientation, where the Sun is (below the horizon, in the direction the crescent's bright side faces). The geometry is legible right there in the shape.

## Not a shadow: clearing the misconception fully

The phases are *not* caused by Earth's shadow, and the case is worth making airtight, because this misconception is so common and its correction is so instructive — it's another specimen of a plausible-but-wrong idea that the real geometry demolishes.

### What the shadow idea claims, and why it fails

The misconception holds that the dark part of the Moon is the *Earth's shadow* cast upon it — that as the Earth moves between Sun and Moon, its shadow sweeps across the Moon's face, producing the phases. Here is why this cannot be right. **First, the Sun and a partly-lit Moon are often visible together.** You can frequently see a crescent or gibbous Moon in the sky *while the Sun is also up.* But to cast a shadow on the Moon, the Earth would have to be *between* the Sun and Moon — which requires the Sun and Moon to be on *opposite* sides of the Earth, and thus never in the same sky at once. Since we *do* see them together, the Moon's darkness *cannot* be Earth's shadow. This is the decisive everyday observation. **Second, the phases happen every month; the shadow alignment doesn't.** The phase cycle runs smoothly and continuously *every* month, but the Earth passes directly between Sun and Moon only *occasionally* — and when it does, we get a lunar eclipse, a distinct and relatively rare event. **Third, the shadow's shape is wrong.** Earth's shadow is round, and during a lunar eclipse it *does* appear as a round bite taken out of the Moon — but the *phases* don't look like that. The terminator (the line dividing light from dark on the Moon) during the phases is a smooth curve that sweeps across as a crescent-to-gibbous progression consistent with viewing a lit sphere from the side — not the circular edge of a shadow.

### The right picture, and the eclipse distinction

The correct picture, again: the Moon is *always* half-lit by the Sun, and the phases are the changing *fraction of the lit half we see* as the Moon orbits and our viewing angle changes. No shadow is involved in the phases at all. The Earth's shadow *does* fall on the Moon — but only occasionally, at certain full Moons, producing a **lunar eclipse.** It's crucial to keep these separate:

- **Phases:** the monthly cycle, caused by our changing view of the half-lit Moon. Happens every month. No shadow.
- **Lunar eclipse:** an occasional event when Earth's shadow actually falls on the (full) Moon, darkening it. Happens a few times a year at most. A real shadow.

Conflating them is the root of the misconception. The phases are geometry-of-viewing-angle; the eclipse is geometry-of-shadow. **Understanding that the phases have nothing to do with shadows — that they are purely about *which fraction of the lit half faces us* — is the key insight of lunar geometry, and another case where the real geometry overturns the plausible-but-wrong first guess.**

## Two kinds of month: sidereal and synodic

Here is a subtle and lovely fact that trips up almost everyone, and that reveals something deep about the interplay of the Moon's orbit and the Earth's orbit: **the time it takes the Moon to go through one full cycle of phases is longer than the time it takes the Moon to actually orbit the Earth once.** These are two genuinely different periods, and understanding why they differ is a beautiful piece of geometric reasoning.

### The two months defined

The **sidereal month** is the Moon's *true orbital period* — the time for the Moon to complete one orbit around the Earth *relative to the stars* (to return to the same position against the background of distant stars). It's about **27.3 days.** This is how long the Moon actually takes to go around the Earth once. The **synodic month** is the *phase cycle* — the time from one new Moon to the next (or full to full) — the period we experience as "a month" of lunar phases. It's about **29.5 days.** The synodic month is about *two days longer* than the sidereal month. The Moon completes an actual orbit in 27.3 days — so why does it take 29.5 days to get back to the same phase?

### Why the phase cycle is longer

The answer is that *the phase depends on the Moon's angle relative to the **Sun**, not relative to the stars — and while the Moon orbits the Earth, the Earth (with the Moon) is also moving around the Sun, so the Sun's direction keeps shifting.* Start at new Moon: the Moon is between Earth and Sun, lined up with the Sun's direction. Now let the Moon orbit. After one *sidereal* month (27.3 days), the Moon has returned to the same position *relative to the stars* — one complete orbit. But during those 27.3 days, the Earth (carrying the Moon along) has traveled about 1/13 of the way around the Sun — so the *direction to the Sun has shifted* by about 27°. The Moon, having returned to the same point against the stars, is *not* yet lined up with the Sun again — the Sun has "moved on." To get back to new Moon, the Moon must travel a bit *further* in its orbit — about two extra days' worth — to catch up to the Sun's new direction. **So the phase cycle (synodic month) is longer than the orbit (sidereal month), because the Moon has to "chase" the moving Sun-direction to return to the same phase.**

```example
synodic-month
```

There's a clean relation capturing this — the same "beat frequency" logic that appears whenever two cyclic motions combine: the synodic rate is the *difference* of the orbital rate and the Earth's yearly rate, $1/T_{\text{synodic}} = 1/T_{\text{sidereal}} - 1/T_{\text{year}}$. Plugging in the sidereal month (27.32 days) and the year (365.25 days) gives a synodic month of 29.53 days — exactly the observed phase cycle. The two-day gap is precisely the time the Moon needs to make up for the Sun's yearly drift.

### Why it matters

This distinction is not mere pedantry. *Practically:* calendars based on the Moon (many ancient and religious calendars) use the *synodic* month of 29.5 days, which is why lunar months are 29 or 30 days and why lunar calendars drift against the solar year. *Conceptually:* it's a lovely illustration that "how long does the Moon take to go around?" has *two different answers* depending on what you measure against — the stars (sidereal, the true orbit) or the Sun (synodic, the phase cycle) — and that the difference between them encodes the Earth's own motion around the Sun. The phase cycle you watch is a *combination* of two motions: the Moon around the Earth, and the Earth around the Sun. The Moon's month carries the fingerprint of the Earth's year. (This same sidereal-vs-synodic distinction applies to the Earth's own rotation — the difference between the sidereal day and the solar day — and to the planets; it's a general feature of nested cyclic motions.)

## Why we always see the same face: tidal locking

Now to one of the most striking facts about the Moon, and one that contains its own delicious subtlety: **we always see the same face of the Moon.** In all of human history, until spacecraft went there, no human ever saw the far side of the Moon — the same familiar face, with the same pattern of dark "seas" and bright highlands, has looked down on every human who ever lived. Why? The answer is called **tidal locking**, and understanding it correctly requires clearing up a subtle point.

### The fact, and the common confusion

The Moon keeps one hemisphere permanently turned toward the Earth; the other hemisphere (the "far side") never faces us. Now, many people conclude from this that *the Moon does not rotate* — that it keeps one face toward us because it's not spinning at all, like a ball held fixed. **This is wrong, and the truth is more elegant: the Moon keeps one face toward us precisely *because* it rotates — it rotates exactly *once per orbit.***

Here's why rotating once per orbit keeps one face toward us. Picture the Moon going around the Earth. If the Moon did *not* rotate at all (kept a fixed orientation in space, like a non-spinning ball), then as it went around its orbit, it would present *all* its sides to the Earth in turn — we'd see the whole surface over one orbit. For the Moon to keep the *same* face toward the Earth, it must *rotate* — turning on its axis exactly once for each orbit it makes, so that its rotation keeps pace with its revolution and the same hemisphere stays pointed inward. **The Moon rotates once every 27.3 days (its rotation period) and orbits once every 27.3 days (its orbital period) — these two periods are *equal*, and that equality is what keeps one face toward us.**

The classic demonstration: walk in a circle around a chair, always keeping your face toward the chair. To do this, you must *turn your own body* once per lap — a friend watching from outside the circle sees your front, then your side, then your back, then your other side; you rotate once per lap. Yet the chair only ever sees your face. You are "tidally locked" to the chair: rotating once per revolution, keeping one face toward it. The Moon does exactly this around the Earth.

### Why the periods are equal: tidal locking

But *why* are the Moon's rotation and orbital periods exactly equal? This is not a coincidence; it's the result of a physical process called **tidal locking**, which acts over long times. The Earth's gravity raises a slight *tidal bulge* on the Moon (stretching it a little along the Earth-Moon line — the same kind of tidal stretching the Moon raises on Earth's oceans). Long ago, the Moon rotated *faster* than it orbited. But when a body rotates faster than it orbits, its tidal bulge gets carried slightly ahead of the Earth-Moon line, and the Earth's gravity pulling back on that bulge creates a *braking torque* that slows the Moon's rotation. This braking continues until the rotation slows to *match* the orbital period — at which point the bulge stays aligned with the Earth-Moon line, the torque vanishes, and the rotation is "locked" to the orbit. **Tidal locking is the natural end-state of this braking process: the Moon's rotation was slowed by Earth's tides until it rotated exactly once per orbit, keeping one face toward us forever after.** This is extremely common in the solar system — most large moons are tidally locked to their planets, because tidal braking, given billions of years, reliably produces synchronous rotation. The Moon showing us one face is not a fluke but the expected outcome of tidal physics.

### The far side is not the "dark side"

One more correction, because it's another common misconception. The hemisphere we never see is often wrongly called the "dark side of the Moon." It is *not* dark. The far side receives *just as much sunlight* as the near side over the course of a month — in fact, at *new* Moon (when the near side faces away from the Sun and is dark to us), the *far* side is fully sunlit. The far side has days and nights just like the near side; it's simply the side that never faces *Earth.* The correct term is the **far side**, not the dark side — "far" (from us) not "dark" (unlit). It was first seen when the Soviet Luna 3 spacecraft photographed it in 1959, revealing a surface that looks intriguingly *different* from the near side (more heavily cratered, with far fewer of the dark volcanic plains called *maria* that make the "man in the Moon" pattern on the near side) — a genuine scientific surprise that we could only discover by going there.

{{image: Far side of the Moon | The far side of the Moon, first photographed by the Soviet Luna 3 in 1959. It is not the "dark side" — it gets just as much sunlight as the near side, and is fully lit at new Moon — merely the side that never faces Earth. It looks strikingly different: heavily cratered, with almost none of the dark volcanic plains that form the "man in the Moon."}}

## The Moon as humanity's first ruler to the cosmos

There's a historical payoff to the Moon's geometry that connects this chapter to the deepest theme of the whole program — that *the sky gives us directions but not distances.* The Moon, it turns out, was humanity's *first stepping-stone* toward measuring the cosmos: the first object beyond the Earth whose real size and distance we figured out, using nothing but phase-and-eclipse geometry and some clever reasoning. The story belongs to a Greek astronomer named **Aristarchus of Samos**, around 270 BC, and it is one of the most brilliant pieces of reasoning in the history of science.

**Sizing the Moon with a lunar eclipse.** Aristarchus realized that a *lunar eclipse* — when the Earth's shadow falls across the Moon — is a cosmic measuring opportunity. During the eclipse, you can watch the *curved edge of Earth's shadow* sweep across the Moon and compare its size to the Moon's. The Earth's shadow at the Moon's distance is roughly two and a half times the width of the Moon — which means the Moon's diameter is a definite *fraction* of the Earth's (about one-quarter to one-third). This was the first time anyone had measured the size of another world *in terms of the Earth* — and the answer (the Moon is roughly a quarter of Earth's diameter) is essentially correct (the true ratio is 0.27).

**Finding the Moon's distance.** Once you know the Moon's true *size* (relative to Earth) and you measure its *angular* size in the sky (about half a degree), simple geometry gives its *distance*: an object of known size subtending a known angle sits at a calculable distance. The Greeks found the Moon to be roughly *30 Earth-diameters* away — remarkably close to the true value. With eclipse and angular-size geometry alone, the ancients had measured both the *size* and the *distance* of the Moon — the first real cosmic distances ever determined.

```example
sun-distance
```

**Reaching for the Sun.** Aristarchus then attempted something even more audacious: measuring the distance to the *Sun*, using the phase geometry itself. His insight was gorgeous. At the exact moment of a *half* Moon (first or last quarter), the Sun-Moon-Earth angle *at the Moon* is exactly 90° (that's what makes it half-lit as seen from Earth). So the Earth, Moon, and Sun form a right triangle with the right angle at the Moon. If you then measure the angle in the sky *between* the Moon and the Sun at that instant, trigonometry gives the ratio of the Sun's distance to the Moon's. Aristarchus measured that angle to be about 87°, concluding the Sun was about 19 times farther than the Moon. His *method* was flawless; his *measurement* was off, because the true angle is about 89.85° — agonizingly close to 90°, far too close for the naked eye to measure precisely — and the real ratio is about 390, not 19. But here is what matters: even his underestimate showed that **the Sun is *much* farther away, and therefore much *larger*, than the Moon** — indeed far larger than the Earth. And that conclusion planted a revolutionary seed: if the Sun is so enormous, might it not make more sense for the tiny Earth to orbit the giant Sun, rather than the reverse? Aristarchus, alone among the ancients, proposed exactly that — a *heliocentric* cosmos, eighteen centuries before Copernicus — and the reasoning that led him there began with the geometry of the Moon.

The lesson: the Moon was humanity's first ruler laid against the cosmos — the object through which we first escaped the flat sky of *directions* and began to measure real *sizes and distances* in three dimensions. The eclipse gave the Moon's size; the angular size gave its distance; the half-Moon geometry reached (however imperfectly) toward the Sun. This is the very first rung of what will eventually become the great *cosmic distance ladder* — and it starts *here*, with the Moon. When we said the sky gives directions but not distances, and that recovering the distances would be one of the great quests of astronomy — *this is where that quest begins.* Every distance humanity has ever measured, out to the farthest galaxy, traces its lineage back to someone watching the Earth's shadow cross the Moon, and reasoning.

## Reading the Moon: phase tells you the time

Here is a genuinely useful and satisfying payoff of the phase geometry — a piece of practical sky-reading: **the Moon's phase tells you *when* it is up in the sky — its rise and set times — and conversely, seeing where the Moon is at a given time tells you its phase.**

### The logic: phase = angle from the Sun = time offset

A Moon's phase is set by its *angle from the Sun* (as seen from Earth): new Moon is *at* the Sun's direction (0°), full Moon is *opposite* the Sun (180°), quarters are 90° away. Now combine this with the daily sky: the Sun rises in the east, is highest at noon, sets in the west — and any object's rise/set time depends on its angular position relative to the Sun. Since the Moon's phase *is* its angular position relative to the Sun, the phase directly determines when the Moon rises and sets.

```moonclock
```

Walk through the phases: the **new Moon** (0° from Sun) rises with the Sun (~6 am), is up during the *day*, and sets with the Sun (~6 pm) — near the Sun, lost in its glare, its dark side toward us anyway. The **first quarter** (90° from Sun, trailing it) rises around *noon*, is highest at *sunset*, and sets around *midnight* — the Moon you see high in the evening. The **full Moon** (180° from Sun, opposite it) rises as the Sun *sets* (~6 pm), is highest at *midnight*, and sets as the Sun *rises* (~6 am) — up *all night*, which is why "the Moon rises as the Sun sets" is specifically a *full*-Moon phenomenon. The **last (third) quarter** (90° on the other side) rises around *midnight*, is highest at *sunrise*, and sets around *noon* — the Moon of the predawn sky.

**So the phase is a clock: tell me the phase, and I'll tell you when the Moon is up.** A crescent low in the west after sunset must be a *waxing* crescent (young Moon, just behind the Sun); a Moon high overhead at midnight must be *full*; a half-Moon visible in the morning sky must be *last* quarter. You can read the Moon's phase and its position together and know exactly where it is in its orbit and its cycle — the whole geometry, legible at a glance.

### Why the Moon rises ~50 minutes later each day

One more elegant consequence. The Moon rises at a noticeably *different* time each night — in fact, about **50 minutes later each day** on average. Why? Because the Moon is *orbiting the Earth*, moving eastward against the stars by about 13° per day (360° in ~27.3 days). So each day, the Moon has shifted about 13° eastward — and the Earth has to rotate an extra 13° to bring the Moon back to the same position. Earth rotates 15° per hour, so an extra 13° takes about 13/15 of an hour ≈ **52 minutes.**

```example
moonrise-delay
```

That's why moonrise marches later day by day, and why the Moon seems to "drift" eastward through the sky over the weeks — you're watching its orbital motion, one 13° step per day, written in the changing time of moonrise. **The daily ~50-minute delay in moonrise is the direct, visible signature of the Moon's orbital motion** — the same motion that drives the phases, showing up as a shift in the clock.

## Three lovely details: earthshine, libration, and the receding Moon

Three refinements deepen the picture and reward the careful sky-watcher.

**Earthshine: the old Moon in the new Moon's arms.** Look carefully at a thin crescent Moon, and you may notice that the *dark* part of the Moon is not perfectly black, but faintly, ghostly visible, glowing with a soft ashen light — you can sometimes see the *whole* circular disk: the bright crescent, plus the dimly-glowing remainder. This sight is called **earthshine**, and its explanation is lovely. The dark part of the Moon is being faintly lit — not by the Sun directly, but by *sunlight reflected off the Earth.* When the Moon is a thin crescent to us (nearly new), an astronaut standing on the Moon's near side would see a nearly *full Earth* blazing in their sky — a big, bright, blue-white disk reflecting lots of sunlight. That reflected earthlight softly illuminates the Moon's night side, making it faintly visible to us. **Earthshine is Earth acting as a mirror** — and it's a two-way geometric mirror of the phases: when the Moon is "new" to us, the Earth is "full" to the Moon, and vice versa. Leonardo da Vinci was the first to correctly explain it, purely by reasoning out the geometry of reflected light.

{{image: Earthshine | Earthshine — "the old Moon in the new Moon's arms." The bright crescent is direct sunlight; the whole dark disk glows faintly with sunlight reflected off the Earth onto the Moon's night side. When the Moon is a thin crescent to us, the Earth is nearly full in the Moon's sky, casting the most earthlight of all.}}

**Libration: we see a bit more than half.** We say we always see the same face of the Moon — but that's not *quite* the whole truth. Over time, we actually get to see about **59%** of the Moon's surface (not just 50%), because of a subtle wobble called **libration.** The Moon appears to rock very slightly back and forth (and up and down) over its cycle, letting us "peek" a little way around its eastern and western edges, and over its north and south poles. This happens because, while the Moon's *rotation* is perfectly steady, its *orbital speed* varies (the orbit is slightly elliptical, so the Moon speeds up and slows down) — so the rotation and the orbital position drift slightly in and out of step, and we glimpse a bit around the sides. A slight tilt of the Moon's axis lets us peek over the poles too. **Libration means the sharp line between "near side" and "far side" isn't perfectly fixed — we see 59% of the Moon over time.**

**The Moon is drifting away.** Finally, a fact that connects the Moon's present to its deep past and future: **the Moon is slowly moving away from the Earth**, receding at about 3.8 centimeters per year (measured with astonishing precision by bouncing lasers off the reflectors that Apollo astronauts left on the surface). This is another consequence of tides: the Moon raises tidal bulges on the *Earth*, and the gravitational interaction between these bulges and the Moon gradually transfers rotational energy and angular momentum from the Earth's spin to the Moon's orbit — slowing the Earth's rotation (the day is lengthening by about 1.7 milliseconds per century) and pushing the Moon into a larger, more distant orbit. So in the deep past the Moon was much closer (and loomed larger in the sky, and the day was shorter); in the far future it will be more distant. **The Moon we see is a snapshot of a slowly evolving system** — locked to us in rotation, drifting from us in distance, its tides reshaping both worlds' motions over the eons. (This slow recession also means that total solar eclipses will eventually cease, in the far future, as the Moon shrinks in our sky.)

## The shadow of a lit sphere

Gather the argument, because this chapter completes our reading of the sky's three great clocks — daily, yearly, and now monthly.

It began with the same Moon in a different shape — the Moon's unique, monthly transformation through crescent, quarter, gibbous, and full — and confronted the common misconception that the phases are the Earth's shadow falling on the Moon. That's wrong: we routinely see the Sun and a partly-lit Moon *together* in the sky (impossible if Earth were casting a shadow between them), and the phases cycle *every* month while Earth's shadow reaches the Moon only occasionally (a lunar eclipse, a separate and rarer event).

The true geometry is a half-lit sphere, seen from a moving point. The Moon is *always* exactly half-lit by the Sun (a permanent day side and night side), and as it orbits the Earth, we view that unchanging lit hemisphere from continuously changing angles — seeing a changing *fraction* of it. New Moon: lit side faces away. Quarter: we see the lit side edge-on (half-disk). Full: lit side faces us fully. The phases are not the Moon changing but *our view* of the half-lit Moon changing, with the lit side always bulging toward the Sun.

We distinguished the two months. The *sidereal* month (27.3 days) is the Moon's true orbit relative to the stars; the *synodic* month (29.5 days) is the phase cycle. The phase cycle is longer because phase depends on the Moon's angle to the *Sun*, and while the Moon orbits, the Earth carries it around the Sun — so the Moon must travel two extra days to "catch up" to the shifted Sun-direction. The Moon's month carries the fingerprint of the Earth's year.

We solved the puzzle of the same face — tidal locking. We *always* see one hemisphere of the Moon, not because it doesn't rotate, but because it rotates *exactly once per orbit* (rotation period = orbital period = 27.3 days) — a locking produced over eons by Earth's tides braking the Moon's spin until it matched its orbit. And the "dark side" is a misnomer: the far side is fully, regularly sunlit (fully lit at new Moon) — it's *far*, not dark, and it looks strikingly different, as Luna 3 first revealed in 1959. Along the way, the Moon became humanity's first ruler laid against the cosmos — Aristarchus's eclipse-and-phase reasoning gave the first real sizes and distances beyond the Earth, the first rung of the cosmic distance ladder.

And we made the geometry practical — reading the Moon as a clock (new hides in the day; first quarter rules the evening; full is up all night; last quarter haunts the predawn), and understanding the ~50-minute daily delay in moonrise as the direct signature of the Moon's 13°-per-day orbital motion. We savored three details: *earthshine* (Earth as a mirror, a full Earth shining on a new Moon), *libration* (the wobble that lets us see 59% of the surface), and the *receding Moon* (drifting away 3.8 cm/year).

The Moon's phases — the most watched, most calendar-defining, most poetically loaded of all sky phenomena — are, like everything else in this course, the *shadow of a geometry.* Not a literal shadow (that misconception we destroyed), but the deeper sense: a simple three-dimensional arrangement — a spherical Moon, half-lit by the Sun, orbiting the Earth, viewed from a moving vantage point — whose changing appearance *is* the phases. This is the through-line, now applied to the third clock: *the daily sky is the shadow of Earth's spin; the yearly sky is the shadow of Earth's tilt; the monthly sky is the shadow of a lit sphere orbiting in space.* Three rhythms, three geometries, one skill — reading the geometry from the appearance. And next comes what happens on the special occasions when the geometry lines up *exactly*: eclipses — the Moon's shadow sweeping across the Earth, and the Earth's shadow swallowing the Moon.

## Further reading

Any introductory treatment of the lunar phases and the Moon's orbit will serve — but best of all, go outside and watch. Note the Moon's phase and where it sits relative to the Sun over a week or two: the crescent near the Sun at dusk, the full Moon rising opposite the setting Sun a fortnight later. Nothing on the page teaches the phase geometry like seeing it play out overhead.

## Problems

The Moon-and-phases set. Problems 1–2 cover the phase geometry and the shadow misconception (conceptual cores); 3 the two months; 4 tidal locking (the other core); 5–6 the far side and reading the Moon as a clock; 7 the 50-minute delay; 8 the subtleties; 9 synthesis.

**1 — The geometry of phases. (Conceptual core.)** **(a)** State the two fixed facts and the one changing quantity that produce the phases. **(b)** For each of new, quarter, and full Moon, describe the Sun-Moon-Earth angle and what fraction of the lit side we see. **(c)** Define waxing and waning, and explain which side of the Moon is lit (and how the crescent's orientation reveals the Sun's direction).

**2 — Not a shadow. (Conceptual core.)** **(a)** State the misconception about what causes the phases. **(b)** Give three reasons the phases cannot be caused by Earth's shadow (include the "Sun and Moon seen together" argument). **(c)** Distinguish the phases from a lunar eclipse — what causes each, and how often does each happen?

**3 — The two months.** **(a)** Define the sidereal month and the synodic month, and give their lengths. **(b)** Explain why the synodic month (phase cycle) is *longer* than the sidereal month (orbit), using the "chasing the moving Sun-direction" reasoning. **(c)** Which month do lunar calendars use, and why?

**4 — Tidal locking. (Conceptual core.)** **(a)** State the fact: what do we always see, and what do we never see? **(b)** Correct the common confusion: does the Moon rotate? Explain why keeping one face toward us *requires* rotation (use the walk-around-a-chair demonstration). **(c)** What are the Moon's rotation and orbital periods, and what is their relationship? **(d)** Briefly explain the mechanism of tidal locking — why did the periods become equal?

**5 — The far side.** Explain why "dark side of the Moon" is a misnomer. When is the far side fully lit? How does the far side differ in appearance, and how do we know?

**6 — Reading the Moon as a clock. (Practical.)** **(a)** For each of new, first quarter, full, and last quarter, state approximately when the Moon rises, is highest, and sets. **(b)** If you see a half-Moon high in the sky at sunset, what phase is it? If you see a half-Moon in the morning sky, what phase? **(c)** Explain why the full Moon (and only the full Moon) rises as the Sun sets.

**7 — The 50-minute delay.** **(a)** The Moon rises about 50 minutes later each day. Explain why, connecting it to the Moon's orbital motion. **(b)** The Moon moves about 13° per day eastward against the stars. Show that this produces roughly a 50-minute daily delay in moonrise (Earth rotates 15° per hour).

**8 — Earthshine and libration.** **(a)** Explain earthshine: what lights the dark part of a crescent Moon, and why is it brightest near new Moon? What does an astronaut on the Moon see at that time? **(b)** Explain libration: why do we see about 59% of the Moon's surface rather than exactly 50%?

**9 — Synthesis (the three clocks).** In three or four paragraphs, explain how the Moon's phases fit the course's through-line, and how the daily, yearly, and monthly chapters together form a trilogy of "the sky read as geometry." Cover: the phase geometry (a half-lit sphere seen from a moving point); why it is *not* a shadow; the sidereal/synodic distinction and what it reveals about combined motions; and tidal locking. Connect the daily sky (Earth's spin), the yearly sky (Earth's tilt), and the monthly sky (the lit sphere orbiting) as three rhythms, three geometries, one skill.

## Worked answers

**1 — The geometry of phases.** **(a)** Two fixed facts: (1) the Moon is *always* exactly half-lit by the Sun (a permanent day side facing the Sun and a night side facing away — the lighting never changes); (2) the Moon *orbits* the Earth (~monthly). The one changing quantity: our *viewing angle* on the lit hemisphere — set by the Sun-Moon-Earth angle, which changes as the Moon orbits. The phases are the changing *fraction of the lit half we see.* **(b)** **New Moon:** angle ~0° (Moon between Earth and Sun); the lit hemisphere faces *away*, so we see ~none of it (dark). **Quarter Moon:** angle ~90° (Moon off to the side); we see the lit hemisphere *edge-on* — half lit, half dark — a *half-disk*. **Full Moon:** angle ~180° (Moon opposite the Sun); the lit hemisphere faces us *fully* — a complete bright disk. **(c)** *Waxing* = lit fraction growing (new → full); *waning* = shrinking (full → new). The lit portion always bulges *toward the Sun* (the light's direction), so a crescent's bright side and horns reveal the Sun's direction: the bright edge faces the Sun (an evening waxing crescent has its lit side toward the just-set Sun in the west; horns point away from the Sun).

**2 — Not a shadow.** **(a)** The misconception: the phases are caused by the *Earth's shadow* falling on the Moon. **(b)** Three reasons it's wrong: (1) *We often see the Sun and a partly-lit Moon in the sky at the same time* — but for Earth to cast a shadow on the Moon, Earth must be *between* Sun and Moon, putting them on opposite sides of us (never seen together). Since we do see them together, the darkness isn't Earth's shadow. (2) *The phases cycle every month*, but Earth passes directly between Sun and Moon only occasionally — when it does, we get a lunar eclipse, a separate rare event. (3) *The shape is wrong* — the phase terminator is the smooth curve of a half-lit sphere seen at an angle, not the round edge of Earth's circular shadow. **(c)** *Phases:* caused by our changing *view* of the always-half-lit Moon as it orbits; happen *every month*; no shadow. *Lunar eclipse:* Earth's shadow *actually* falling on the (full) Moon at a precise alignment; happens only *occasionally*; a real shadow.

**3 — The two months.** **(a)** *Sidereal month* (~27.3 days): the Moon's true orbital period — one orbit relative to the *stars.* *Synodic month* (~29.5 days): the phase cycle — new Moon to new Moon. **(b)** The synodic month is longer because *phase depends on the Moon's angle to the **Sun**, not the stars* — and while the Moon orbits the Earth (27.3 days), the Earth carries the Moon about 1/13 of the way around the Sun, so the Sun's direction shifts ~27°. After one sidereal orbit (back to the same star), the Moon is *not* yet realigned with the Sun, so it must travel ~2 extra days to catch up and return to the same phase. (Relation: $1/T_{\text{syn}} = 1/T_{\text{sid}} - 1/T_{\text{year}}$ → 29.5 days.) **(c)** Lunar calendars use the *synodic* month (~29.5 days), because the phase cycle is what people actually observe. That's why lunar months are 29–30 days and lunar calendars drift against the solar year.

**4 — Tidal locking.** **(a)** We always see the *same hemisphere* (the near side); we never see the far side from Earth (until spacecraft). **(b)** Yes, the Moon *rotates* — and keeping one face toward us *requires* it to rotate. If the Moon did *not* rotate (fixed orientation in space), then as it went around its orbit it would present all its sides to Earth in turn. To keep the *same* face toward Earth, it must turn on its axis once per orbit. Walk-around-a-chair demo: to circle a chair always facing it, you must rotate your body once per lap (an outside observer sees front/side/back/side), yet the chair only sees your face. **(c)** The Moon's rotation period (~27.3 days) *equals* its orbital period (~27.3 days) — synchronous rotation — which keeps one face toward us. **(d)** Tidal locking: Earth's gravity raises a slight tidal bulge on the Moon. Long ago the Moon rotated faster than it orbited, carrying its bulge ahead of the Earth-Moon line; Earth's gravity pulling back on that bulge created a braking torque that slowed the Moon's rotation — until it matched the orbital period, aligning the bulge and zeroing the torque. The rotation became "locked" to the orbit. (A common outcome of tidal evolution over billions of years.)

**5 — The far side.** "Dark side" is a misnomer because the far side receives *just as much sunlight* as the near side over a month — it's not unlit, just never facing *Earth.* It's fully lit at *new* Moon (when the near side faces away from the Sun and is dark to us, the far side faces the Sun and is fully sunlit). The correct term is *far side.* It differs in appearance: more heavily cratered, with far fewer dark volcanic plains (*maria*) than the near side. We know this because spacecraft photographed it — first the Soviet Luna 3 in 1959 — revealing the surprising difference.

**6 — Reading the Moon as a clock.** **(a)** **New Moon:** rises ~sunrise (~6 am), highest ~noon, sets ~sunset (~6 pm) — up during the day (invisible). **First quarter:** rises ~noon, highest ~sunset, sets ~midnight — up in the evening. **Full Moon:** rises ~sunset (~6 pm), highest ~midnight, sets ~sunrise (~6 am) — up all night. **Last quarter:** rises ~midnight, highest ~sunrise, sets ~noon — up in the predawn/morning. **(b)** A half-Moon *high at sunset* is a **first quarter** (up in the evening). A half-Moon *in the morning sky* is a **last (third) quarter** (up in the predawn). **(c)** The full Moon rises as the Sun sets because it is *opposite* the Sun (180°): when the Sun is on the western horizon setting, the point opposite it is on the eastern horizon rising. Only the full Moon does this, since only then is the Moon opposite the Sun.

**7 — The 50-minute delay.** **(a)** The Moon rises ~50 minutes later each day because it is *orbiting the Earth*, moving eastward against the stars by ~13° per day. So from one day to the next, the Moon has shifted ~13° eastward, and the Earth must rotate an *extra* ~13° to bring the Moon back to the rising position — which takes extra time, delaying moonrise. It's the visible signature of the Moon's orbital motion. **(b)** The Moon moves ~13° per day eastward (360°/27.3 days ≈ 13°/day). Earth rotates 15° per hour. The extra 13° of rotation takes $13°/(15°/\text{hour}) = 0.87$ hour ≈ **52 minutes** — matching the observed ~50-minute daily delay.

**8 — Earthshine and libration.** **(a)** *Earthshine:* the dark part of a crescent Moon is faintly lit by *sunlight reflected off the Earth* (Earth as a mirror). It's brightest near new Moon because that's when the Moon is a thin crescent to us — and correspondingly, from the Moon, the Earth appears nearly *full* (a big, bright, blue-white disk reflecting maximal sunlight), casting the most earthlight onto the Moon's night side. An astronaut on the Moon's near side then sees a brilliant nearly-full Earth. (A mirror-image of the phases: new Moon to us = full Earth to the Moon.) **(b)** *Libration:* we see ~59% (not 50%) of the surface over time because the Moon appears to rock slightly back and forth (and nod up and down), letting us peek around its edges and over its poles. Main cause: the Moon's *rotation* is steady but its *orbital speed varies* (elliptical orbit), so rotation and orbital position drift slightly in and out of step; a slight axial tilt lets us peek over the poles. So the near/far boundary isn't perfectly fixed.

**9 — Synthesis, the three clocks.** This chapter completes a trilogy: with the daily and yearly chapters, it shows the three great rhythms of the sky — daily, yearly, and monthly — each as the shadow of a simple geometry, read from the appearances. The Moon's phases, the most watched and calendar-defining of all sky phenomena, turn out (like the daily turning of the stars and the yearly cycle of seasons) to be not what they naively seem, but the visible expression of a three-dimensional arrangement in space. The phase geometry is beautifully simple: the Moon is always exactly half-lit by the Sun, and as it orbits the Earth, we view that unchanging lit hemisphere from continuously changing angles — seeing none of it (new), half of it edge-on (quarter), or all of it (full). The phases are not the Moon changing but *our view* of the half-lit Moon changing. Crucially, the phases are *not* a shadow — dismantling that misconception is as instructive here as demolishing the "distance causes seasons" error was earlier. We routinely see the Sun and a partly-lit Moon together (impossible if Earth cast a shadow between them), and the phases cycle every month while Earth's shadow reaches the Moon only occasionally. And the geometry yields rich consequences: the *sidereal* month (the true orbit, 27.3 days) differs from the *synodic* month (the phase cycle, 29.5 days) because phase depends on the angle to the moving Sun, so the phase cycle carries the fingerprint of the Earth's orbital motion; and *tidal locking* explains the same-face puzzle — the Moon rotates *exactly once per orbit*, locked by eons of Earth's tidal braking. Seen together, the daily, yearly, and monthly chapters form one continuous lesson in reading the sky as geometry. The daily sky is the shadow of *Earth's spin*; the yearly sky is the shadow of *Earth's tilt* carried around its orbit; the monthly sky is the shadow of *a lit sphere orbiting the Earth, seen from a moving point.* Three rhythms, three geometries — and one skill running through all of them: refusing to take the appearance at face value, and instead reconstructing the three-dimensional arrangement that *casts* it. In each case a plausible-but-wrong reading (the stars really move; the seasons come from distance; the phases are a shadow) gives way to the true geometry, which is simpler, deeper, and *legible* once you see it. That is the through-line of the course: *every apparent motion in the sky is the shadow of a real geometry, and astronomy is the art of reading the geometry from the shadow.*$astro3_master$,
