ry month while Earth's shadow reaches the Moon only occasionally (a lunar eclipse), and the phase terminator is the smooth curve of a lit sphere seen at an angle, not the round edge of a shadow."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why is the synodic month (the phase cycle, ~29.5 days) LONGER than the sidereal month (the Moon's true orbit, ~27.3 days)?", "options": ["The Moon slows down over the month", "Because a phase depends on the Moon's angle to the SUN, not to the stars \u2014 while the Moon completes one orbit relative to the stars, the Earth carries it ~1/13 of the way around the Sun, so the Sun's direction shifts and the Moon must travel ~2 extra days to line up with the Sun again and return to the same phase", "Because the Moon's orbit is elliptical", "Because the Earth's rotation adds two days"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The sidereal month (27.3 days) is one true orbit, measured against the stars. But the phase is set by the Moon's angle to the Sun, and during those 27.3 days the Earth carries the Moon about a thirteenth of the way around the Sun, shifting the Sun's direction by ~27\u00b0. Having returned to the same place among the stars, the Moon is not yet re-aligned with the Sun and must travel ~2 extra days to catch up \u2014 giving the 29.5-day synodic month. The phase cycle you watch carries the fingerprint of the Earth's own year (1/T_syn = 1/T_sid \u2212 1/T_year)."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "We always see the same face of the Moon. Does the Moon rotate, and why is the far side not the 'dark side'?", "options": ["The Moon does not rotate at all; and the far side is genuinely never sunlit", "The Moon DOES rotate \u2014 keeping one face toward us REQUIRES it to rotate exactly once per orbit (rotation period = orbital period = 27.3 days), a synchrony produced by tidal locking; and the far side gets just as much sunlight as the near side (it is fully lit at new Moon) \u2014 it is 'far' from us, not dark", "The Moon rotates twice per orbit; the far side is dark because it faces away from the Sun", "The Moon wobbles randomly; the far side is dark because it is colder"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "If the Moon did not rotate, it would show us every side over one orbit; to keep the SAME face inward it must turn on its axis exactly once per orbit \u2014 so it rotates, and its rotation and orbital periods are equal (27.3 days), the hallmark of tidal locking (Earth's tides braked the Moon's spin until it matched its orbit). Walk around a chair always facing it and you must turn once per lap. The hidden hemisphere is fully, regularly sunlit \u2014 it is fully lit at new Moon \u2014 so it is the FAR side, not the dark side."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why does the full Moon (and only the full Moon) rise just as the Sun sets?", "options": ["Because the full Moon is closest to the Earth", "Because a phase IS the Moon's angle from the Sun, and the full Moon is opposite the Sun (180\u00b0): when the Sun is on the western horizon setting, the point opposite it is on the eastern horizon rising \u2014 so the full Moon rises exactly at sunset and is up all night", "Because the full Moon orbits faster than other phases", "Because the Sun pushes the Moon up as it sets"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Moon's phase is its angular position relative to the Sun, and that fixes its rise/set times. The full Moon sits 180\u00b0 from the Sun, directly opposite it, so as the Sun sets in the west the full Moon rises in the east, climbs highest at midnight, and sets at sunrise \u2014 up the whole night. Only the full Moon does this, because only then is the Moon opposite the Sun. (The new Moon, at the Sun's direction, rises and sets with the Sun; the quarters, 90\u00b0 away, rise at noon or midnight.)"}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Build the geometry of the lunar phases from scratch \u2014 the two fixed facts and the one changing quantity \u2014 and use it to explain new, quarter, and full. Then dismantle the misconception that the phases are the Earth's shadow, giving the reasons it fails and distinguishing the phases from a lunar eclipse.", "rubric": "THE TWO FIXED FACTS: (1) the Moon is ALWAYS exactly HALF-LIT \u2014 it is a sphere that shines only by reflecting sunlight, and the Sun (far to one side) illuminates whichever hemisphere faces it: a permanent DAY side and NIGHT side, at every moment, regardless of phase; the lighting of the Moon NEVER changes. (2) the Moon ORBITS the Earth (~monthly), so its position relative to the Sun\u2013Earth line continuously changes. THE ONE CHANGING QUANTITY: our VIEWING ANGLE on that unchanging lit hemisphere \u2014 set by the Sun\u2013Moon\u2013Earth angle, which changes as the Moon orbits. The phases are the changing FRACTION of the lit half that we can see. THE KEY POSITIONS: NEW MOON \u2014 Sun\u2013Moon\u2013Earth angle ~0\u00b0 (Moon between Earth and Sun); the lit hemisphere faces AWAY from us, the dark side toward us, so we see ~none of it (dark/invisible). QUARTER MOON \u2014 angle ~90\u00b0 (Moon off to the side); we see the lit hemisphere EDGE-ON, half the lit side and half the dark side facing us, giving a HALF-DISK (called 'quarter' because the Moon is a quarter of the way through its cycle, not because we see a quarter of the disk). FULL MOON \u2014 angle ~180\u00b0 (Moon opposite the Sun); the lit hemisphere faces us FULLY, a complete bright disk. In between: CRESCENT (a sliver, new\u2192quarter) and GIBBOUS (most of it, quarter\u2192full); WAXING = lit fraction growing, WANING = shrinking; the lit portion always bulges TOWARD the Sun (so a crescent's bright side/horns reveal the Sun's direction below the horizon). DISMANTLING THE SHADOW MISCONCEPTION: the claim is that the dark part of the Moon is the EARTH'S SHADOW cast on it. It fails because: (1) we OFTEN SEE THE SUN AND A PARTLY-LIT MOON TOGETHER in the sky \u2014 but casting a shadow on the Moon requires the Earth to be BETWEEN Sun and Moon, putting them on OPPOSITE sides of us, never visible together (the decisive everyday observation); (2) the phases cycle SMOOTHLY EVERY MONTH, while the Earth passes directly between Sun and Moon only OCCASIONALLY; (3) the SHAPE is wrong \u2014 the phase terminator is the smooth curve of a half-lit sphere seen at an angle, not the round edge of Earth's circular shadow. PHASES vs LUNAR ECLIPSE: PHASES are caused by our changing VIEW of the always-half-lit Moon as it orbits \u2014 happen EVERY month, NO shadow. A LUNAR ECLIPSE is when Earth's shadow ACTUALLY falls on the (full) Moon at a precise alignment \u2014 happens only OCCASIONALLY (a few times a year at most), a REAL shadow. Conflating the two is the root of the misconception: the phases are geometry-of-viewing-angle, the eclipse is geometry-of-shadow. A strong answer stresses the phases involve NO shadow at all \u2014 just which fraction of the lit half faces us \u2014 and notes this is another case where the real geometry overturns a plausible-but-wrong first guess."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Explain tidal locking \u2014 why we always see one face, why this REQUIRES the Moon to rotate, and the physical mechanism that made its spin and orbit synchronous. Then explain how the Moon became humanity's first rung on the cosmic distance ladder, via Aristarchus's reasoning.", "rubric": "THE FACT: we always see the SAME hemisphere of the Moon (the near side, with its familiar pattern of dark maria and highlands); the FAR side never faces Earth (unseen until spacecraft \u2014 Soviet Luna 3, 1959). WHY IT REQUIRES ROTATION (the common confusion corrected): it is WRONG that the Moon doesn't rotate. If the Moon did NOT rotate (kept a fixed orientation in space), then as it went around its orbit it would present ALL its sides to Earth in turn (we'd see the whole surface each orbit). To keep the SAME face toward Earth, it must turn on its axis exactly ONCE per orbit, its spin keeping pace with its revolution. So its ROTATION period (~27.3 days) EQUALS its ORBITAL period (~27.3 days) \u2014 synchronous rotation \u2014 and that equality is what keeps one face toward us. (Walk-around-a-chair demo: to circle a chair always facing it you must rotate your body once per lap \u2014 an outside observer sees front/side/back/side \u2014 yet the chair only sees your face.) THE MECHANISM (tidal locking): Earth's gravity raises a slight TIDAL BULGE on the Moon. Long ago the Moon rotated FASTER than it orbited, which carried the bulge slightly AHEAD of the Earth\u2013Moon line; Earth's gravity pulling back on that leading bulge created a BRAKING TORQUE that slowed the Moon's rotation \u2014 until it slowed to MATCH the orbital period, aligning the bulge with the Earth\u2013Moon line, zeroing the torque, and LOCKING the rotation to the orbit. Given billions of years this reliably produces synchronous rotation, which is why MOST large moons are tidally locked. (Aside: the far side is NOT the 'dark side' \u2014 it gets just as much sunlight, fully lit at new Moon \u2014 it is far, not dark.) THE FIRST RUNG OF THE DISTANCE LADDER (Aristarchus, ~270 BC): the Moon was the first world beyond Earth whose real SIZE and DISTANCE we measured, from the phase-and-eclipse geometry \u2014 the beginning of escaping the flat sky of mere DIRECTIONS (the L1 theme: the sky gives directions but not distances). (a) SIZE: during a LUNAR ECLIPSE, watch the curved edge of Earth's SHADOW sweep across the Moon and compare sizes \u2014 the shadow is ~2.5\u00d7 the Moon's width, so the Moon is a definite fraction (~1/4 to 1/3) of Earth's diameter (true 0.27) \u2014 the first size of another world measured in Earth-units. (b) DISTANCE: knowing the Moon's true size and measuring its ANGULAR size (~0.5\u00b0), simple geometry gives its distance \u2014 ~30 Earth-diameters (close to true). (c) THE SUN: at a HALF (quarter) Moon the Sun\u2013Moon\u2013Earth angle at the Moon is exactly 90\u00b0, so Earth\u2013Moon\u2013Sun form a RIGHT TRIANGLE; measuring the sky-angle between Moon and Sun then gives the RATIO of their distances (Sun/Moon = 1/cos angle). Aristarchus measured ~87\u00b0 \u2192 Sun ~19\u00d7 farther; the true angle is ~89.85\u00b0 (too close to 90\u00b0 for the naked eye) \u2192 ~390\u00d7, so his number was far too SMALL \u2014 but his METHOD was flawless, and even the underestimate proved the Sun is MUCH farther and therefore MUCH LARGER than the Moon, far larger than the Earth. From that he made the revolutionary leap that the tiny Earth might orbit the giant SUN \u2014 a HELIOCENTRIC cosmos eighteen centuries before Copernicus. A strong answer ties it to the through-line: the cosmic distance ladder \u2014 every distance humanity has ever measured \u2014 traces back to reasoning from the geometry of the Moon; this is where the quest to recover the sky's missing third dimension (distance) begins."}]$astro3_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/foundations/observed-sky/phases', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
